#!/bin/bash
#
# Tell every running marquee-markdown to re-read the terminal's colors.
#
# Omarchy runs `theme-set.d` hooks last, after the terminal configs have been
# rewritten and the terminals told to reload them — so by the time this runs,
# asking a terminal what colors it is using gets the new answer rather than the
# old one. That ordering is the whole reason to install this rather than rely
# on the other triggers: focus regain and a watched path can both fire while
# the terminal is still repainting, and have to probe-and-compare to cope.
#
# Install with:
#
#   install -Dm755 packaging/omarchy/theme-set.d/reload-marquee \
#     ~/.config/omarchy/hooks/theme-set.d/reload-marquee
#
# Nothing here is Omarchy-specific except the directory it lives in. Any
# desktop that can run a command after retinting its terminals can send the
# same signal.
#
# Both binaries, because a reader may have started either: they are two real
# binaries rather than a symlink, so only the one that was actually run is
# there to signal.
#
# `marquee-markdow` is not a typo. `pkill -x` matches the kernel's process
# name, which is capped at 15 characters — `marquee-markdown` is 16, so
# spelling it in full matches nothing at all and `pkill` says so on standard
# error. `-f` against the whole command line would avoid the truncation and
# introduce a worse problem: it would match any editor, pager or shell with
# the string in its arguments, and signal those instead.
#
# A run with no reader open exits 1, which is not a failure worth reporting,
# hence the explicit success below.

pkill -USR1 -x marquee-markdow
pkill -USR1 -x mmd

exit 0
